p(19,16,"I will not tell about our way back. The doctor was a heavier",
 "On the way back, the doctor lets the horse breathe, then Joe makes a mistake: he does not put the warm cloth on the hot horse and gives cold water. Black Beauty gets very cold and ill, and John comes and helps him.",
 "I will not tell about our way back. The doctor was a heavier man than John and not so good a rider, but I did my very best. The man at the tollgate had it open. When we came to the hill, the doctor pulled me in and said, “Now, my good fellow, take some breath.” I was glad he did, because I was nearly worn out, but that rest helped me on, and soon we were in the park. Joe was at the lodge gate, and my master was at the hall door, because he had heard us coming. He did not say a word. The doctor went into the house with him, and Joe led me to the stable. I was glad to get home. My legs shook under me, and I could only stand and breathe hard. I did not have a dry hair on my body, the water ran down my legs, and I steamed all over, like a pot on the fire, as Joe used to say. Poor Joe was young and small, and at that time he knew very little, and his father, who would have helped him, had been sent to the next village. But I am sure he did the best he knew. He rubbed my legs and my chest, but he did not put my warm cloth on me, because he thought I was so hot that I would not like it. Then he gave me a pail of water to drink. It was cold and very good, and I drank it all. Then he gave me some hay and some corn, and thinking he had done right, he went away. Soon I began to shake and tremble, and I turned very cold. My legs ached, my loins ached and my chest ached, and I felt sore all over. How I wished for my warm, thick cloth as I stood and trembled! I wished for John, but he had eight miles to walk. So I lay down in my straw and tried to go to sleep. After a long time I heard John at the door. I gave a low moan, because I was in great pain. He was at my side in a moment, bending down by me. I could not tell him how I felt, but he seemed to know it all. He covered me with two or three warm cloths and then ran to the house for hot water. He made me some warm gruel, which I drank, and then I think I went to sleep.",
 ["drew me up","spent","pant","steamed","pailful","loins","ached","moan","stooping","gruel"])
s(19,"I wanted no whip nor spur, and for two miles I galloped as fast as I could lay my feet to the ground",
 "","") if False else None
